#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" :question/math-academy-id "e-21665" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = y^{2}(t) - x(t)$\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 0; y^{2} - x = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 0⇒y = \\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{2})^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0$, we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0$.\n- For $x=4$, we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/id #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" :question/math-academy-id "e-21666" :question/problem "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n1. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n2. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n3. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n4. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O$, approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty$.In plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$, remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it's not stable.In plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\n> Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822ac31f-fd59-5c24-bd6a-a144699796af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220317} {:answer/id #uuid "b7718c17-8459-5fd0-97e2-8b47804c7caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220318} {:answer/id #uuid "e530f5de-7137-569f-b10a-053e324c9cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220319} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b49f0b-9676-55b0-b673-8bf536b71cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220320} {:answer/id #uuid "3563a3f9-53d2-5c0f-94f3-70ffefcfa11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b49f0b-9676-55b0-b673-8bf536b71cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220320} :answer-field/id #uuid "2326d025-2caa-5e2b-b5a5-5b29e6091c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220316}] :question/id #uuid "8eda104b-fb43-5560-b213-0b2f03bde928" :question/math-academy-id "q-333360" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c549d0ed-1c30-5a3a-b716-f720ad9aa9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220337} {:answer/id #uuid "d07e7a83-3509-5d50-b258-7b5d720fec78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable only for certain initial conditions." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220338} {:answer/id #uuid "01bf4b62-ca90-52d9-ae81-52349884e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220339} {:answer/id #uuid "1231d677-4871-51fa-b89f-2fde1c562f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220340} {:answer/id #uuid "548bc11d-36ec-562d-921e-af30373bf967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Stable but not asymptotically stable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c549d0ed-1c30-5a3a-b716-f720ad9aa9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Unstable." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220337} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e5af361-3180-5bd7-aef9-d46cae03651f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220336}] :question/id #uuid "1b4d4a22-e4b9-59fa-a122-f0172b49e4a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333385" :question/problem "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral away from the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?"}]}
